{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.55,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "short_to_supply",
      "components": ["U22"],
      "pins": ["U22.4", "U22.2", "U22.1"],
      "nets": ["+3V3", "ESTOP_SINK1", "GND"],
      "observed": "U22 (SN74LVC1G07, open-drain buffer) has its output pin Y (pin 4) on the +3V3 net, and its data input A (pin 2) on GND. Its /OE (pin 1) is on ESTOP_SINK1. When ESTOP_SINK1 goes low, the buffer drives its output low, pulling the +3V3 rail to ground. The other e-stop sinks (U15, U35, U36) route Y to a signal net (Net-(U15-Y), ESTOP_SINK_ALL, ESTOP_OUT) rather than to a supply rail.",
      "expected": "The e-stop sink buffer output should drive a signal net, as with U15, U35 and U36, and should not be tied to the 3.3 V supply. A constant-low A input driving the supply rail is a short path.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(node (ref \"U22\") (pin \"4\") (pinfunction \"Y\") (pintype \"output\")) (node (ref \"U22\") (pin \"5\") (pinfunction \"VCC\") (pintype \"power_in\"))"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(node (ref \"U22\") (pin \"1\") (pinfunction \"/OE\") (pintype \"input\"))"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "E-stop sink/buffer circuit feeding `ESTOP_OUT`... `U15`, `U22`, `U35`, `U36` (`SN74LVC1G07`) wired as `/OE` gates on `ESTOP_SINK1`/`ESTOP_SINK2`/`ESTOP_SINK_LOCAL`"}
      ],
      "impact": "When the ESTOP_SINK1 line is pulled low, the 3.3 V rail is shorted to ground through the buffer output, which can brown out or damage the 3.3 V domain and disable the e-stop logic it feeds.",
      "confidence": 0.5
    },
    {
      "category": "wrong_component_type",
      "components": ["D29", "R46"],
      "pins": ["D29.1", "D29.2", "R46.1", "R46.2"],
      "nets": ["Net-(D29-K)", "/LED_3.3V_POWER_NONINV", "+3V3"],
      "observed": "The red LED D29 has its anode on /LED_3.3V_POWER_NONINV and its cathode, through R46, on +3V3. Both ends of the LED therefore sit at the 3.3 V level, so it is never forward-biased and cannot light. The other LEDs on the same anode net (D8, D28) return their cathodes to a switched signal or sink net.",
      "expected": "Each indicator LED should have its cathode returned to a sink or signal node so that it can be forward-biased when the enable net is asserted.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(node (ref \"D29\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"A\") (pintype \"passive\"))"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(node (ref \"R46\") (pin \"2\") (pintype \"passive\")) (node (ref \"R46\") (pin \"1\") ..."}
      ],
      "impact": "The LED D29 never illuminates, so the status indicator is missing and the pin-to-rail connection is not what a working indicator needs.",
      "confidence": 0.3
    },
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R36"],
      "pins": ["R36.1", "R36.2"],
      "nets": ["VOUTN", "VOUTP"],
      "observed": "R36 is valued 24k, but its library part and MPN describe a 24 Ω part (description \"24Ω 0805\", MPN FRC0805J240TSD, where 240 means 24 Ω). A 24 kΩ value would be expected for a bleed of this kind, so the value field and the purchased part disagree by three orders of magnitude.",
      "expected": "The schematic value should match the MPN and description of the fitted part. A 24 Ω resistor across a 12 V-class output would dissipate far more than a 0805 can handle.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(comp (ref \"R36\") (value \"24k\")"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(description \" 125mW Thick Film Resistors 150V ±100ppm/℃ ±5% 24Ω 0805 Chip Resistor - Surface Mount ROHS\")"}
      ],
      "impact": "If the part is fitted as the MPN states, the resistor is 1000 times too low, creating a heavy load on VOUTP/VOUTN and overheating the part.",
      "confidence": 0.3
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The most likely anomaly is U22's open-drain output tied to the +3V3 rail while its input is tied to GND, unlike the other e-stop sinks, which drive signal nets. Lower-confidence findings are an LED cathode returned to +3V3 (D29) and an R36 value that conflicts with its 24 Ω part number."
}

